WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.560
My knitting instructions are telling
me to increase stitches evenly across

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.120
the row. In this sample,
I've removed the knitting from the machine

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.040
and then I'm rehanging the stitches,
leaving empty needles.

4
00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:19.520
The first method is a hand knitting
technique called knit in the front and back.

5
00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:25.000
It's also called M1 or make one.
Worked this way,

6
00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:28.320
it's a left-leaning increase.

7
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:31.720
This method is often used
for decorative increases.

8
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:35.040
It's fiddly to do on the machine.

9
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:40.880
Pick up the right side of the stitch
with a transfer tool and place that strand

10
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:47.640
on the empty needle, keeping the original
stitch on its original needle.

11
00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:11.200
You can see how the stitch is twisted.

12
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:20.840
When it's knit, you can see that the leg
of this increase is leaning left.

13
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:25.720
Another increase you can use as a standard

14
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:30.880
machine knitting increase.
Pick up the purl bump of the row below

15
00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:36.360
and fill in the empty
needle for the increase.

16
00:02:05.840 --> 00:02:10.040
As you can see, both of these
methods give very different results.

